A||N||3||sparks||Securing speedo gear?||||5||
Z||000000||sparks||03-02-2001||11:35 AM||||So has anyone come up with a way to secure the speedo gear to the VSS on Isuzu transaxles so that you can check your fluid without fear of dropping the gear?<P>It just seems like such a hassle to me to have to take that out just to check the fluid every time and worry about dropping the gear in.  I just got a new transaxle in my 85 because I chewed up the gear that drives the speedo gear inside the transaxle.  Consequently I'm a bit timid to even just check the fluid level, let alone change it.<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||5
Z||000001||TheFiero64||03-02-2001||11:39 AM||||On my 88 the gear is pressed on. It is hard to pull off. I've had a 4 speed with a loose one and I put a little electric tape on it and pushed the gear on so it was tight.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000002||bHooper||03-02-2001||11:47 AM||||are you talking about the plastic gear inside the tranny, or the red cog attached to the pick up sensor?<P>if it's the cog, all i can say is be more carefull.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||sparks||03-02-2001||12:09 PM||||It's the red cog.  I knocked it off twice, the second time I was being carefull.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>But in the process I managed to chew up the plastic gear in the tranny and a new (used) tranny was cheaper than dissasembling and reassembling the tranny to put in a new plastic drive gear.<P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||11
